{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Visiting the iniquity of fathers upon children and upon children's children, upon the third and upon the fourth.</b> This verse we find in the Torah in four places: two in the two Decalogues, the first and the second, and two among the attributes of the Holy One, blessed be He — one in Ki Tissa, and the second in the portion of Shelach in the matter of the spies. Those in the Decalogues are a visitation of vengeance, to annihilate them and blot out their name from the world, for it is written there 'to those who hate Me'; but the two among the attributes of the Holy One, blessed be He, are a visitation of punishment, to increase and multiply upon them sufferings, so that they may serve as atonement for their sins and in order to benefit them in their end." "<b>And indeed,</b> the fact that the Holy One, blessed be He, visits the iniquity of the father upon the children is cause for wonder, for it appears that this is not just judgment, seeing that the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded in the Torah (Deuteronomy 24): 'Fathers shall not be put to death for children, nor children for fathers; each man shall be put to death for his own sin.'" "<b>But</b> this matter our Rabbis, of blessed memory, already explained to us: this verse of 'visiting the iniquity of fathers' speaks when the children hold fast to the deeds of their fathers in their hands. And Scripture notes that this visitation extends four generations, and the reason is that it is possible for a person's days to be prolonged and to see four generations, in the manner written in Job (Job 42): 'and he saw his sons and his sons' sons, four generations.' And likewise it is written of Joseph (Genesis 50): 'And Joseph saw for Ephraim children of the third generation' — the word 'children' attached, and they are the fourth. And Scripture informed us how far the boundary of kinship reaches: for the compassion of the fathers extends this far and no further. Therefore the wicked man who saw his father and his father's father and his grandfather, who were doers of wickedness, and behold he too follows after them in that path and holds fast to wickedness like them — it is fitting to bring sufferings upon him and to visit upon him the iniquity of them all. And here the questioner asks: since the measure is thus, that this visitation is up to four generations and no more, if so, why does Scripture say (Exodus 32), 'and on the day of My visitation, I will visit upon them'? And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: there is no generation that does not have in it a 'spasm' (אונקיא) from the iniquity of the calf; and in another formulation they said elsewhere: there is no punishment that comes upon Israel that does not contain a little of the punishment of the calf. If so, how does the Holy One, blessed be He, visit upon them in every single generation a bit of that iniquity with the measure of visitation, when even when they hold fast to the deeds of their fathers in their hands it does not extend except to four generations? And the answer to this is that what is said, 'I will visit,' is not the language of visitation here but the language of remembrance: the Holy One, blessed be He, remembers the iniquity of the calf when the generation sins, but does not visit it, for in the destruction of the Temple everything was visited and the iniquity was wiped out entirely, for certainly that destruction and that enormous loss that Israel suffered served to purge their iniquities, so that nothing of the iniquity of the calf remained to them at all. And behold, we find regarding the destruction of the Temple that it is written (Ezekiel 9): 'Draw near, you punishers (פקודות) of the city, and each man with his destroying weapon in his hand' — and in the word פקודות this verse alludes to 'and on the day of My visitation, I will visit.'" "<b>And likewise</b> I found in the Midrash of Lamentations: 'How long does the sin of the calf endure? R. Berakhiah says: Until the calves of Jeroboam, as it is said (Hosea 7), 'He heals Israel, and the iniquity of Ephraim is uncovered.' R. Shmuel bar Nahmani says: Until the destruction of the Temple, as it is said, 'Draw near, you punishers of this city.' From this you learn that the iniquity of the calf is not visited at all from the destruction of the Temple onward. And even though it is not visited, it is remembered — the Holy One, blessed be He, remembers it. And this is what our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded in the chapter 'Ein dorshin': When the Temple was destroyed, the wings of the living creatures that cover their feet with them were diminished, as it is said (Ezekiel 1), 'and the sole of their feet was like the sole of a calf's foot.' If they were not diminished, from where would He know it? Perhaps it was revealed and shown to Him? But if you say thus regarding 'and the likeness of their faces was the face of a man' — so too this should have been diminished! Rather, it was revealed... and so on. Now accordingly: the face — it is the way of the world to reveal before one's Master; but the feet — it is not the way of the world to reveal before one's Master. Therefore the wings of the feet were diminished, and the reason was in order to recall the iniquity of the calf. And I will further mention, in the commandment of tzitzit, the number of wings that were diminished. If so, behold the iniquity is remembered — for if He did not remember it, He would conduct Himself with them according to the measure of mercy, and that remembrance holds back the mercy. But in the time to come He will forget it entirely, as He promised us through His prophet (Isaiah 49): 'These too may forget, yet I will not forget you.'" "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah,</b> 'visiting the iniquity of fathers upon children' — this is the secret of עבור (the act of passing/transgression). For upon the wicked He may visit times three, together with the man, and with Him they are four. And this judgment is the matter of the thoughts that the woman of Tekoa said to David — that the Holy One, blessed be He, devises (II Samuel 14), 'so that none banished may be banished from Him.'" "<b>And you should</b> acquire understanding to grasp the forceful teaching in Sefer ha-Bahir: R. Rchumai said: The Light preceded the creation of the world, as it is said (Genesis 1): ״יהי אור ויהי אור״. They said to Him: Before the formation of Your son You make a crown for him. He said to them: To what may the matter be compared? To a king who desired a son, and he found a beautiful crown and praised it, saying: 'This is for my son's head.' They said to him: 'But is it known that your son is worthy?' He said to them: 'Be silent.' Thus it arose in thought and was known, as it is said: ״וחשב מחשבות לבלתי ידח ממנו נדח״. End of quote. And this is the lovingkindness of HaShem, may He be exalted, toward Israel — that they not be lost from the World to Come, but that all may merit before Him. And they stated explicitly: All Israel have a portion in the World to Come, as it is said (Isaiah 60): ״ועמך כלם צדיקים לעולם יירשו ארץ״. And to this mystery King Solomon, peace be upon him, alluded in his saying (Ecclesiastes 1): ״מה שהיה הוא שיהיה״, etc., and he said (ibid.): ״דור הולך ודור בא״, etc. For by way of the plain sense (peshat) it would have been fitting to say ״דור בא ודור הולך״, since those who are born are coming and those who die are going; but he said ״דור הולך ודור בא״ — for the one going is coming. And this is the language of דור, which is from the language of (Isaiah 22): ״כדור אל ארץ רחבת ידים״, and this is clear."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:07:14.588123027Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}